## ABSTRACT
While research on building integrated photovoltaics (BIPVs) has mainly focused on power-generating window applications, the utilization of other underutilized surface areas in buildings, including exteriors, facades, and rooftops has still not been fully explored. The most important requirements for BIPVs are color, power conversion efficiency (PCE), and long-term stability. In this work, we achieved colorful (red, green, blue, RGB) perovskite solar cells (PSCs) with minimized PCE loss (<10%) and enhanced photostability by exploiting the optical properties of non-periodic multi-nanolayer, narrow-bandwidth reflective filters (NBRFs). The NBRFs were fabricated by multi-layering high-index \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)/low-index \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) in non-periodic manner, which allowed devices to demonstrate various colors with effectively suppressed unwanted baseline ripple-shape reflectance. The PCE of PSCs with non-periodic RGB-NBRFs were 18.0, 18.6, and 18.9%, which values represent reductions of only 10, 7, and 6% of PCE values, respectively, compared to a black control PSC (20.1%). Moreover, the photostability of the PSCs was substantially improved by using the NBRFs because of ultraviolet (UV) blocking in the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layers. The G-PSC retained 65% of the initial PCE after 60 h of continuous illumination (AM 1.5G one sun) at maximum power point (MPP), whereas black PSC retained only 30%. Aesthetic color value, low PCE loss, and enhanced photostability of PSCs were simultaneously achieved by employing our NBRFs, making this a promising strategy with potential applicability in power-generating building exteriors.
KEYWORDS: colorful perovskite solar cell, optical interference effects, narrow-bandwidth reflective filters, non-periodic multi-nanolayer, long-term stability
Since the pioneering discovery by Miyasaka et al. in 2009,\(^{1}\) organic-inorganic metal halide-based perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have received much attention as promising next-generation solar cells owing to their high efficiencies and low-cost solution-processability.\(^{2-5}\) Large absorption coefficients (\(>10^{5}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\)), high charge carrier mobilities (\(\sim10\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}/\mathrm{V}\cdot\mathrm{s}\)), tunability of optical properties, and optimization of device structure enable the PSCs to reach a certified power conversion efficiency (PCE) of \(24.2\%\).\(^{6}\) Recently, the potential of PSCs for use in building-integrated photovoltaics (BIPVs) has been suggested owing to their excellent combination of high PCE, low-cost solution processability, and color tunability compared to other thin-film solar cells such as amorphous silicon (a-Si),\(^{7}\) cadmium telluride (CdTe),\(^{8}\) organic solar cells (OSC),\(^{9}\) and copper indium gallium diselenide (CIGSe).\(^{10}\) Studies of PSCs for BIPVs have mainly focused on power-generating window applications; however, the full potential of PSCs has still not been fully explored for BIPV applications.\(^{11-13}\)
Applications of solar cells in BIPVs are not limited solely to power-generating windows but also include exteriors,\(^{14}\) facades,\(^{15}\) and rooftops.\(^{16}\) While current commercial Si-based solar modules are typically mounted on rooftops, other underutilized surface areas account for more than twice the area of rooftops. These areas can provide a tremendous opportunity for additional installation of solar cells to achieve the ultimate goal of buildings with net-zero energy consumption.\(^{17}\) One of the most important requirements for BIPVs is the color of the solar cells because the colors of building exteriors demonstrate the traditional, cultural, and religious features of cities. However, despite their high PCEs, it is intrinsically difficult to the colors of commercial Si-based solar cells. In response to these challenges associated with Si-based solar cells, color-tunable PSCs have begun to emerge as cost-effective candidates that can be printed in high-throughput manufacturing processes.\(^{18-20}\)
Thus far, strategies reported for the color tuning of PSCs, such as composition tuning,\(^{21}\) plasmonics,\(^{22}\) and photonic crystal\(^{23}\) and micro-cavity\(^{24}\) strategies, have focused on
the development of semi-transparent PSC devices for power-generating windows. However, colored PSCs exhibit significantly lower PCEs compared to their conventional uncolored forms (black color). The use of a multi-nanolayered reflective color-generated filter is another strategy to tune the color of solar cells without modification of their photoactive layers.\(^{9,25,26}\) In our earlier works, multi-nanolayered cut-off dichroic filters or narrow-bandwidth reflective filters (NBRFs), realized by periodic alternating deposition of low-refractive-index \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) and high-refractive-index \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), were used to tune the color of copper indium gallium sulfide selenide (CIGSSe) solar cells.\(^{27,28}\) Using a periodic \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-based cut-off filter, red and yellow CIGSSe solar cells were achieved, although their PCEs were reduced by a factor of \(>40\%\) due to the large overlap between the reflective spectra of the filters and the absorption of the CIGSSe solar cells.\(^{27}\) Recently, a series of full-color NBRFs (notch-type filters) was also developed to produce red (R), green (G), and blue (B) CIGSSe solar cells while retaining small PCE drop with the help of an anti-reflection effect.\(^{28}\)
To apply PSCs for long-term use BIPVs, sufficient device stability should be secured under various environmental conditions such as chemical, heat, moisture, and ultraviolet (UV) irradiation. While the chemical, heat, and moisture stabilities of PSCs can possibly be improved by employing encapsulation techniques, the UV stability of PSCs cannot be greatly improved by encapsulation. In general, the absorption of UV light is inevitable for solar cells. Recently, various endeavors to filter UV light and enhance long-term UV stability of devices have been attempted.\(^{29-31}\) The formation of UV blocking layers has been the simplest approach: one method involves introducing UV absorbing material, another uses UV reflective layers on PSCs.\(^{32,33}\) Development of a strategy that can avoid UV absorption with minimal PCE loss will be an essential milestone for the commercialization of PSCs.
In this work, color generation and UV cut-off in NBRFs were achieved by developing non-periodically high-index \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)/low-index \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) multi-nanolayers. The non-periodic NBRFs
demonstrated R, G, and B colors with negligible baseline ripple-shape reflectance. Using the NBRFs, colorful PSCs (R, G, B) with minimal PCE loss \((<\!10\%)\) and improved photostability were achieved. The PCEs of the R-PSC, G-PSC, and B-PSC devices were 18.0, 18.6, and \(18.9\%\) , respectively, while that of a conventional black PSC was \(20.1\%\) . Pearl-like colors were demonstrated in the PSCs owing to the color change with respect to the viewing angle.28 Moreover, the photostability of the PSCs was substantially improved by using the NBRFs because of the UV block in the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layers. The PSC with G-NBRF retained \(65\%\) of the initial PCE after \(60\,\mathrm{h}\) under continuous illumination (AM 1.5G one sun) at maximum power point (MPP), whereas black PSC retained only \(30\%\) .
## Results/Discussion
Various NBRFs were designed to generate assorted pure colors over the visible range, while reduction in PCE of colored PSCs was minimized. Figures 1a and b show schematic diagrams of NBRF color films with two different structures (periodic and non-periodic stacking) of multiple nanolayers. These NBRF films are composed of multiple layers with alternating low-refractive-index \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) (1.45) layers and high-refractive-index \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) (2.31) layers. Various peak wavelengths and full-width at half-maximum (FWHM) values of NBRFs have been demonstrated by manipulating optical interference effects between the high- and low-index layers. By controlling the periodic and non-periodic stacking sequence and the thickness of each high- and low-index layer in the multiple nanolayers, we also simulated seven colors of the two types of NBRFs (Figure 2a – 2b). Violet (V), blue (B), cyan (C), green (G), yellow (Y), orange (O), and red (R) NBRFs were obtained by tuning the peak wavelength. Moreover, we simulated G-NBRFs with various bandwidths by varying the number of stacks and the thicknesses of the high- and low-index layers (Figure 2d – 2e). The
simulated results successfully demonstrated the color-generating capabilities of NBRFs, such as reflected brightness and color-purity with respect to wavelength and bandwidth.
In general, desired peak wavelength and bandwidth of pure-colored NBRFs are sensitively affected by changes in the structural parameters. Particularly, owing to the large index-contrast between \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) (1.46) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) (2.31), narrow-band and pure colors can be achieved by combinatorial optimization of the thickness, stacking order, number of stacking pairs, and periodicity of high- and low-index layers. Among the various structural parameters, the thickness of the high-index \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer in NBRF plays the most important role in determining the bandwidth of both types of NBRFs. Moreover, as the thickness of each multi-nanolayer increases, the peak wavelength of the NBRF shifts to the longer wavelength region. As the number of layers stacked by depositing \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) increases, the shapes of the reflectance spectra of both NBRF types approach rectangular form. Figure 2a shows simulated reflectance spectra of VBCGYOR NBRFs with similar FWHM values (\(\sim50\;\mathrm{nm}\)). Similarly, Figure 2d shows simulated reflectance spectra of G-NBRFs with different FWHM values (30 nm, 50 nm, 70 nm, and 90 nm). These were obtained by using the 'OpenFilters' software to optimize the thickness and numbers of periodically stacked high- and low-index layers (see Table S1). Based on simulation studies of G-NBRF, it is clearly revealed that purer color was obtained from reflective spectra with narrower bandwidth, whereas brighter color was achieved using reflective spectra with wider bandwidth.
As shown in Figures 2a and d, baseline ripple-shape patterns in the reflectance spectra were observed in all studied NBRFs due to the secondary interference effect of the one-dimensional photonic crystal (1D PC) multiple high-/low-layers. This effect decreases the purity of the color generation and create unwanted reflection, which might reduce the PCEs of the PSCs. According to the basic concept of a bandstop filter, the characteristics of
baseline ripple-shape patterns are induced by high secondary constructive interference and equivalent admittance of the system.\(^{34}\) Many researchers have attempted to eliminate baseline ripple-shape patterns,\(^{35}\) and one popular strategy is the to utilize computer simulation. In our simulations, we first used the 'OpenFilters' software to design a periodic array of multi-nanolayer NBRFs; the layer structure was further refined using 'The Essential Macleod,' along with a conjugated gradient method. As a result, the elimination of baseline ripple-shape patterns was achieved by non-periodic stacking. Figures 2b and 2e show the complete elimination of baseline ripple-shape patterns of VBCGYOR NBRFs and G-NBRFs with various bandwidths. Figure 2c compares the color gamut on the CIE color coordinate diagram of the two VBCGYOR NBRFs fabricated by periodic and non-periodic stacking of high-/low-index layers. It can be seen that the color gamut of ripple-free, non-periodic NBRF is broader, with enhanced color purity, than that of control periodic NBRFs.
On the basis of the simulated results, we fabricated non-periodic NBRFs composed of high-index \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and low-index \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\). Figures 3a and 3b show the basic optical properties of the fabricated RGB-NBRFs, as well as their images. The baseline ripple-shape patterns of the fabricated RGB-NBRFs were nearly eliminated, though not completely due to the practical limitations in the fabrication process. The peak wavelengths of the RGB-NBRFs are \(642\;\mathrm{nm}\), \(542\;\mathrm{nm}\), and \(468\;\mathrm{nm}\), respectively. All RGB-NBRFs showed high reflectance (\(>90\%\)) at the stopband wavelength, while they maintained high transmittance (\(>90\%\)) in other wavelengths. Figures 3c and 3d show color coordinate diagrams of the reflectance and transmittance spectra of the simulated and fabricated RGB-NBRFs. As can be seen in Figure 3c, the color gamut of the simulated RGB-NBRFs is broader than that of the fabricated RGB-NBRFs because the reflectance baseline of the fabricated RGB-NBRFs is increased due to the inherent reflectance of the glass surface. In the transmittance spectrum, though it is
difficult to expect transmitted color due to the complementary color of the light, the location of the color coordinates, i.e., the simulated and fabricated transmittance, nearly match, with only a negligible difference (Figure 3d). Figures 3e – 3g show FE-TEM cross-sectional images of FIB-sliced RGB-NBRF specimens. The structures of all RGB NBRFs were well-matched with the simulated structures (thickness and order of stacks), as indicated in Table S2. Particularly, as indicated in Figure S1, the non-periodic array deposition of \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), used to eliminate the ripple-shape patterns, was also successfully achieved, just as in the simulation. Moreover, the increases in the thickness of the high- and low-index layers red-shifted the wavelengths of the NBRF films (Table 1), identical to the simulation results.
It is well known that 1D multi-nanolayers show optical property variation with respect to viewing angle. Therefore, it is expected that the RGB-NBRF films will show different reflected colors depending on the viewing site. Figure 4 shows the gradual change of the reflected color of the RGB-NBRFs with angular variation. According to the reflectance spectra of the RGB-NBRFs shown in Figures 4a – 4c, all spectra are blue-shifted when the viewing angle increases. In addition, the actual color of the RGB-NBRFs changes gradually with the viewing angle, ending in a pearl-like color (Figures 4d – 4f), which reflects the trend in the spectra of the RGB-NBRFs (Figure 4a – 4c). This pearl-like color formation with respect to viewing angle was beneficial from an aesthetic standpoint when our NBRFs were used in BIPV applications. Figure S2 shows the optical properties of four G-NBRFs with respect to viewing angle. Similar to the optical properties of RGB NBRFs at various viewing angles, all spectra were blue-shifted as the viewing angle increased. The effect of the blueshift is more clearly observed at the angle of \(>30^{\circ}\) (Figure S2). In order to investigate the effects of the NBRFs on the performance of PSCs, devices with RGB-NBRFs were fabricated (inset of Figure 6c). Among four samples, the brightest color was obtained from the G-NBRF
with the widest bandwidth (FWHM = 90 nm); the pearl-like reflection was also enhanced as the bandwidth increased. However, the performance of colorful PSCs with NBRFs might have a trade-off between color brightness, purity, and photon absorption; thus we chose an appropriate bandwidth (FWHM = 50 nm) for the NBRFs to realize high performance and aesthetic value.
Regarding the PCE of the PSCs with NBRFs, the peak wavelength and bandwidth of the NBRFs are important because the reflectance properties will reduce device photon absorption. In order to investigate the effects of bandwidth on the performance of PSCs, we fabricated G-NBRFs with various bandwidths. Figure 5 shows the optical properties (5a – 5e) and the morphology (5f – 5i) of the G-NBRFs with various FWHM values. The G-NBRFs with four different bandwidths (30, 50, 70, and 90 nm) were not perfect but nearly matched the designed shapes of the stopbands (Figures 5a and 5b). Among them, the G-NBRF with the narrowest bandwidth (FWHM = 30 nm) showed a slightly blue-shifted spectrum compared to the other samples. Because the thickness difference of the high-and low-index layers of the G-NBRF with narrowest bandwidth is too large, small thickness variations during the fabrication process significantly influenced the peak position and shape of the spectra. The fabrication of high-index/low-index multilayers with large thickness difference was extremely challenging. We attributed the blue-shifted spectrum in the G-NBRF with the narrowest bandwidth to thickness variations during the e-beam co-deposition process. Notably, there are almost no baseline ripple-shape patterns in the reflectance spectra of our NBRFs, which reveals that the non-periodic deposition following the simulation results was successful. The color purity and brightness of reflected spectra generated by the four G-NBRFs show the trade-off relationship. The brightest green color was obtained from the widest G-NBRF and the purest green color was obtained from the narrowest G-NBRF.
However, as shown in Figure 5c, the CIE color coordinates of the reflectance spectra of the G-NBRFs are located at similar positions, irrespective of the bandwidth. Otherwise, as shown in Figure 5e, the color coordinates of the transmitted color of the G-NBRFs systematically shifted to purer violet color with increases in the bandwidth of the G-NBRFs. The large amount of white color created by the baseline reflectance significantly changed the green colors generated by G-NBRFs because the baseline reflectance is sufficiently intensive to have a negative effect on the color change in G-NBRFs. As a result, the change in FWHM of G-NBRF has some influence on the change of color coordinates of G-NBRF in 1931 CIE color space. Otherwise, the change of the complementary color of G-NBRFs strongly depends on the variation of bandwidth owing to the limited influence of transmitted light generated by the baseline reflectance.
Figures 5f – 5i show cross-sectional FE-TEM images of FIB-sliced G-NBRF samples with various bandwidths. The non-periodic structure of alternate stacking of high- and low-index films is confirmed. Due to the large difference in the refractive index between \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), the narrowest G-NBRF, with an FWHM of \(30\;\mathrm{nm}\), required the greatest number of stacking layers and the total thickness corresponded well to the simulated results.
The effects of the color controlled NBRFs on the performance of the colorful PSCs were investigated. Figures S3 shows device architecture with NBRFs and a cross-sectional SEM image of a control PSC. Current density-voltage \((J_{-}V)\) plots under AM 1.5G one sun illumination \((100\;\mathrm{mWcm}^{-2})\) and external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra of control PSC are shown in Figure 6a and 6b, respectively. The PCE of the control PSC device was \(20.1\%\) (\(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(1.15\;\mathrm{V}\), \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of \(23.0\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), FF of 0.76). The \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) values obtained from the \(J_{-}V\) plots of the control PSC were in good agreement with the \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) values estimated from EQE spectra, with \(<3\%\) mismatch (Table 2).
The diffusive reflectance spectra of the PSCs with RGB-NBRFs revealed that the optical properties of the NBRFs were well-reflected in the colors of the devices (Figure 6c). The FWHM of the reflectance spectra of RGB-NBRFs was \(50\;\mathrm{nm}\). The high color purity of the PSCs with RGB-NBRFs was indicated in the CIE x and y color coordinates (Figure 6d). The PCEs of PSCs with non-periodic RGB-NBRFs were 18.0, 18.6, and \(18.9\%\), which values represent losses of only 10, 7, and \(6\%\) respectively (Figure 6e) compared to black control PSC \((20.1\%)\). The device parameters are summarized in Table 2, and their statistical distributions are shown in Figure S4. The use of RGB-NBRF mainly decreased the \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of devices due to blocking of particular wavelengths for optical absorption. The \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) values of the PSCs using RGB-NBRFs, relative to that of the control PSC, were \(91\%\), \(93\%\), and \(93\%\) for the R, G, and B based devices, respectively (Figure 6f). Notably, the PSC with RGB-NBRFs showed lower \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) and higher FF compared to black control PSC (Table 2 and Figure S4). The decreased numbers of incident photons (i.e. reduced charge generation) in PSCs with RGB-NBRFs influenced the quasi-Fermi level separation and ideality factors.\(^{36}\)
To investigate the effects of reflection bandwidth of RGB-NBRFs on the color and performance of PSCs, G-NBRFs with various FWHMs (30, 50, 70, and \(90\;\mathrm{nm}\)), shown in Figure 5a, were employed for PSCs. The color brightness of the G-NBRF based PSCs improved with increases in the bandwidth (inset photo images of Figure 5a). Moreover, the CIE x and y color coordinates of all G-NRRF based PSCs with various bandwidths are consistent (Figure 5b). Figures 7a and 7b show \(J_{\mathrm{v}}\) curves and EQE spectra of G-NBRF based PSCs with various bandwidths; the results are summarized in Table 3. The PCE of the G-NBRF based PSC decreased as the bandwidth increased. The blocking of photons in wider wavelength regimes reduced the \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) values. The colors of devices were altered with respect to the viewing angle, as shown in Figure 4 and Figure S2. To elucidate the effects of the
viewing angle on the device performance, we measured the \( J_{-}V \) characteristics and EQE spectra of G-NBRF based PSCs and control PSCs with respect to viewing angle (Figure S5). In accordance with the results for G-NBRF films (Figure S2), there was blue-shift in EQE of G-NBRF based PSCs as the viewing angle changed from \( 0^{\circ} \) to \( 40^{\circ} \) (Figure S5c). However, there was no significant alteration in device PCE (Figure S5 and Table S3). The control PSC also exhibited nearly identical performance regardless of viewing angle (Figure S5 and Table S3).
While colorful PSCs with negligible sacrifice of PCE were successfully achieved using our NBRFs, the long-term stability of devices is another key issue for commercialization of PSCs. For instance, to apply these colorful PSCs to building exteriors, sufficient photostability against UV-light is required. It is well known that UV-light can induce oxygen radicals, which can generate deep trap states at the interfaces, leading to performance degradation. Several different methods to overcome UV assisted photo-degradation have been demonstrated, including coating with UV-blocking layers. Figure 8a shows the photostability of PSCs without encapsulation under AM 1.5G one sun illumination (100 mWcm\(^{-2}\)) in a glovebox. The PSC using \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NBRF retained \( 81\% \) of its original PCE, while the PSC using control PSC maintained only \( 62\% \). We also measured the long-term stability of PSCs under continuous illumination (AM 1.5G one sun) at MPP for \( 60\,\mathrm{h} \) under ambient air (Figure 8b). The PSC using G-NBRF exhibited substantially improved photostability (\( \sim\!65\% \) of initial PCE after \( 60\,\mathrm{h} \)) compared to the control (\( \sim\!30\% \) of initial PCE after \( 60\,\mathrm{h} \)). We attributed the improved photostability of the PSC with NBRF to UV absorption by \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layers in the NBRFs. To confirm this hypothesis, we also fabricated PSCs using \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) based NBRF. As shown in Figure 8c, the blocking of UV transmission was found in \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NBRF, whereas the conventional \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) based NBRF significantly
transmits in UV region (290 – 370 nm). As a result, the \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) NBRF based PSC demonstrated photostability comparable to that of the control PSC (Figure 8a and Figure S7). This result confirms that the origin of improved photostability of NBRF-based PSC is the efficient UV blocking of our \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NBRF.
## Conclusions
High efficiency colorful PSCs with improved long-term photostability were achieved by exploiting color controlled non-periodic multi-nanolayer NBRFs. To optimize the multi-color NBRFs, their optical properties were first investigated via simulation, and a non-periodic stacking concept with multi-nanolayer deposition was employed for fabrication by e-beam evaporation. While the NBRF-based PSCs demonstrated aesthetic value, with pearl-like color, there was a decline in the PCE. Through the development of non-periodic high-index/low-index stacking architecture for NBRFs, the unwanted baseline ripple-like reflectance was suppressed while minimizing PCE decrease. The PCE values of PSCs with non-periodic RGB-NBRFs were 18.0, 18.6, and \(18.9\%\), which values are reductions of only 10, 7, and \(6\%\), respectively, compared to black control PSC (20.1%). The photostability of PSCs was also substantially improved by using non-periodic NBRFs with UV blocking \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layers. The PSC with G-NBRF retained \(65\%\) of the initial PCE after \(60\,\mathrm{h}\) under continuous illumination (AM 1.5G one sun) at MPP, whereas black PSC retained only \(30\%\). Aesthetic color value, pearl-like color appearance, minimum PCE loss, and enhanced photostability were simultaneously achieved by our developed, nonperiodic \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NBRFs. This result can lead to potential applicability of thin-film solar cells as power-generating building exteriors.
## Methods/Experimental
Simulation and fabrication of RGB color and G-NBRFs with various FWHMs: RGB NBRF and G-NBRFs with various FWHMs were simulated by using the 'Openfilter' software to generate high reflection zones with interference effect; 'Essential Macleod' software was used to eliminate ripple patterns of the reflectance while controlling the thickness and number of stacked high- and low-index layers. Periodic stacked multi-nanolayers (terminal eighth-wave-thick \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) and quarter-wave-thick \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) multi-nanolayer \(((0.5\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/0.5\mathrm{SiO}_{2})^{\mathrm{n}})\) were initially simulated and then were modified via a refinement process that employed a conjugated gradient method. Based on simulated data, RGB NBRFs and G-NBRFs with various FWHMs were fabricated on glass and opposite sides of ITO-coated glass substrates by depositing alternatively high- and low- index materials. Table 1 shows the thicknesses of each NBRF of the resulting \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) multi-nanolayer structures.
Characterization of NBRF films: The structural properties of the RGB NBRF films were investigated using FE-TEM at \(200\,\mathrm{kV}\) (JEM-2100F, JEOL Ltd.). A FIB (XL 30, Philips) process was applied to obtain TEM samples of the RGB NBRF film. All transmittance spectra were measured using an S-3100 device (Scinco Co. Ltd.) and the diffusive reflectance spectra were measured using an LS-F100HS source unit with a 100 W halogen lamp (PSI).
Fabrication of PSC: Before use, indium doped tin oxide coated glass (ITO/glass) substrates were sequentially cleaned using acetone and isopropyl-alcohol. After cleaning, \(\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) bilayer electron transporting layers (ETL) were deposited. The \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) layers and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layers were deposited by in situ sol-gel conversion (thickness of \(40\;\mathrm{nm}/30\;\mathrm{nm}\)). The \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) layers were fabricated using \(0.45\;\mathrm{M}\) solution with annealing at \(130\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\;\mathrm{min}\). The \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer was prepared using a precursor solution (a mixture of \(\mathrm{HCl}\) and titanium(IV) isopropoxide) in
anhydrous isopropanol (IPA, 0.15 M).\(^{40}\) The \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) solution was spin-coated onto ITO/ZnO substrates at 2000 rpm for \(30\;\mathrm{s}\), followed by annealing at \(150\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\;\mathrm{min}\) and subsequently at \(500\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(20\;\mathrm{min}\). The \(\mathrm{MA}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{FA}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Br}_{1-\mathrm{x}})_{3}\) based organic-inorganic hybrid perovskite (OIHP) active layers were prepared via two-step sequential method.\(^{41}\) First, \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) solution was prepared by dissolving \(461\;\mathrm{mg}\) of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) in \(1\;\mathrm{mL}\) of dimethyl-formamide (DMF). Then, the solution was spin-casted at \(1800\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(20\;\mathrm{s}\), followed by thermal annealing at \(100\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(3\;\mathrm{min}\). After cooling down, the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) layers were dipped into a mixed solution of formamidium iodide (FAI)/methyl-ammonium bromide (MABr). To prepare FAI/MABr solution, \(600\;\mathrm{mg}\) of FAI and \(100\;\mathrm{mg}\) of MABr were dissolved in \(10\;\mathrm{mL}\) IPA and heated at \(60\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\;\mathrm{min}\) before use. Then, the films were annealed at \(80\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\;\mathrm{min}\). After cooling, the perovskite films were washed with IPA using drop casting method at \(3000\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(20\;\mathrm{s}\), followed by thermal annealing at \(100\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in vacuum (\(10^{-4}\) Torr) for \(20\;\mathrm{min}\). To prepare hole transport layer (HTL), spiro-OMeTAD was coated over OIHP layers. \(0.6\;\mathrm{mM}\) of spiro-OMeTAD solution was prepared in \(1\;\mathrm{mL}\) of chlorobenzene additive (\(31\;\mathrm{mM}\) of bis(trifluoromethane)sulfonimide lithium (Li-TFSI) and \(190\;\mathrm{mM}\) of tert-butylpyridine (TBP). The entire device fabrication process was performed under air atmosphere with controlled humidity (\(\leq35\%\)). Finally, an Au electrode (thickness of \(100\;\mathrm{nm}\)) was thermally evaporated at reduced pressure (\(\sim10^{-6}\) torr).
Characterization of PSCs: The structural properties of PSC were measured by field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM) (JSM-7610F, JEOL Ltd.). The current density-voltage (\(J_{-}V\)) characteristics were analyzed using a Keithley 2401 source unit; the devices were illuminated by a Xenon lamp solar simulator (Newport) under a light intensity of AM \(1.5\;\mathrm{G}\) (\(100\;\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\)). The light intensity was calibrated by a KG-5 filter-covered monosilicon standard cell (Newport). All devices were measured under ambient air with \(100\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) scanning rate. EQE measurements were performed using QuantX 300 (ORIEL), calibrated
with a 603621 silicon and germanium reference detector. A continuous photo-stability test was performed under AM 1.5G one sun illumination without UV filter. The unencapsulated devices were measured every \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) for \(504\,\mathrm{h}\) at stabilized power output condition. The relative humidity was \(20\%\) and temperature was \(45\pm5\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). For long-term storage stability test, unencapsulated devices were stored in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\)-filled box under dark condition. The devices were measured under AM 1.5G one sun illumination \((100\;\mathrm{mWcm}^{-2})\). The relative humidity was \(20\%\) at room temperature. The devices were measured every \(120\,\mathrm{{h}}\) for more than \(1000\,\mathrm{h}\) of storage.
